Sogen: the NSI service answers change notification registrations

iphlpapi's change notifications -- NotifyIpInterfaceChange,
NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange and their kin -- go through winnsi.dll on
this build: asynchronous NDR64 RPC to the Network Store Interface
service, interface 7ea70bcf-48af-4f6a-8968-6a440754d5fa, over ncalrpc
with no endpoint of its own, so rpcrt4 first asks the endpoint mapper
for one. WinHTTP's transport, webio.dll, registers for both before its
first send, and Sogen ran no NSI service.

Nor could its endpoint mapper have named one. Its ept_map read the
request as two pointers and a handle, so it took the map tower's bytes
for max_towers and answered with them as its array's maximum count; the
client rejects a count that is not its own max_towers, and the send
failed with RPC_X_BAD_STUB_DATA (1783). The request is an object UUID,
the map tower as a twr_t, the entry handle, a null pointer and
max_towers, in both NDR and NDR64.

The endpoint mapper now reads it so, and answers a tower for an
interface Sogen serves with the client's own tower, its endpoint floor
naming the port; any other is still not registered. The NSI port takes
winnsi's registration (opnum 5) and deregistration (opnum 6) with a
success status and keeps the change wait (opnum 7) outstanding: Sogen's
network does not change under the guest, so the notification never
comes. Keeping a call is new for an asynchronous send: a server that
answers STATUS_PENDING queues no reply. NSI's parameter calls carry data
Sogen does not have and are refused.

